---
import SiteFrame from '../components/SiteFrame.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';

const title = 'Why sdlrealtor.com | Scottsdale Realtor Domain';
const description =
  'sdlrealtor.com pairs the Scottsdale airport code SDL with realtor. Eleven letters, no hyphen, a .com built for a luxury Scottsdale real estate brand.';
---

<SiteFrame
  title={title}
  description={description}
  crumbs={[{ name: 'The Name', href: '/scottsdale-realtor-domain/' }]}
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="The name"
    title="A Scottsdale realtor domain people can repeat."
    lede="sdlrealtor.com is for sale because the words already belong together. SDL is local. Realtor is the category. The .com is the one people still type."
  />

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 sm:py-16 space-y-10 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">SDL is already Scottsdale</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Scottsdale Airport uses the identifier SDL (ICAO KSDL). Locals shorten the city the same way.
        That is why a string of sister names — sdl.contact, sdl.life, and others — read as Scottsdale
        before anyone explains them. sdlrealtor.com uses that shorthand on purpose. It does not need
        “Arizona” stuffed into the label, and it does not hide behind a hyphenated slogan.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        The airport reference is a memory hook, not a claim that the domain is connected to the city
        or to
        <a href="https://www.scottsdaleaz.gov/scottsdale-airport" class="font-medium text-slate-900 underline decoration-slate-300 underline-offset-4" rel="noopener noreferrer">Scottsdale Airport</a>.
        It is a naming fact a buyer can use.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Eleven letters, one word, a .com</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        sdlrealtor is eleven characters. No numbers. No hyphen. The extension is .com, which still
        carries more trust on a yard sign, a business card, and a spoken radio spot than a newer
        extension most clients have to spell. Say it once: “S-D-L realtor dot com.” It fits a voicemail.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Exact category language helps people remember you and find you when they already know the name.
        It is a brand asset. It is not a ranking prize. Google stopped handing page-one placements to
        thin exact-match domains years ago. After transfer, visibility still comes from the site,
        the reviews, and the links the new owner earns.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Who the name fits</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        The cleanest owner is a realtor or small team whose work is Scottsdale luxury: North Scottsdale
        estates, golf communities such as Grayhawk and Troon, and buyers comparing the city with
        nearby Paradise Valley. A boutique brokerage can use it as the flagship. A relocation desk or
        a developer can use it as the Scottsdale front door of a larger brand.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        It is a poor fit for a national portal, a property-management mill, or anyone who needs the
        domain to pretend a business already exists. Nothing is attached to the name except the name.
        Market context — high-value homes, second-home buyers, limited desert land — is why the brand
        is worth saying. Those conditions are the city’s, not a promise baked into DNS.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">What you are not buying</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        You are not buying a brokerage, a book of business, or membership in the National Association
        of REALTORS®. REALTOR® is their trademark. Using that mark in a business name has rules,
        including membership. SDL Domains is not affiliated with NAR, the City of Scottsdale, or any MLS.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        You are buying the right to point sdlrealtor.com at whatever you build next, after a normal
        escrow and registrar transfer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p>
      <a href="/acquire/#inquiry" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center h-12 px-6 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900 text-white font-semibold">
        Make an offer on sdlrealtor.com
      </a>
    </p>
  </article>
</SiteFrame>
